<h1>Video 089 — Synchronized block</h1>
<h2>Video info</h2>
<p>Title: Core Java with OCJP/SCJP: Multi Threading Part-9 || synchronized block</p>
<p>Source: real YouTube auto-captions (.transcripts/089-ORzmjiYmOJg.txt). ASR decoded in place: diluk / dagar / D nagar / dilik = Dilsukhnagar; Bomb BL / blurs = bomb blasts; synchronizer = synchronized; threats = threads; Viada / vij / Vijay = Vijayawada; kada / kod = Kodad (narrow bridge place); one lak / Lakh = 1,00,000 lines; Vish / wish = wish; Don / D = Dhoni; Yas / you / your = Yuvi; vo / wi = void; lack = lock; inex / in x = int x; compartive / compar = compile; risk condition = race condition; keep subing = keep writing; inter = interview.</p>
<p>Last session: synchronized method, object-level lock, class-level lock. This session: synchronized block — need, declaration forms, demo vs full sync method, FAQs.</p>
<h3>00:09 — Why synchronized block? (Dilsukhnagar bomb analogy)</h3>
<p>Last session covered synchronization, synchronized method, object-level lock, class-level lock. Now: synchronized block.</p>
<p>Analogy: bomb blasts at Dilsukhnagar (Hyderabad). Worst “programming”:</p>
<ul><li>Because blast at Dilsukhnagar → no flights from New York</li></ul>
<ul><li>No city buses in London</li></ul>
<ul><li>No local trains in Sydney</li></ul>
<p>People would laugh. Correct response: block Dilsukhnagar area (or at most Hyderabad), not the whole world.</p>
<p>Same anti-pattern in code:</p>
<ul><li>Method has ~10,000 lines</li></ul>
<ul><li>Only ~10 lines (e.g. DB update) need synchronization</li></ul>
<ul><li>Many programmers: declare entire method synchronized</li></ul>
<ul><li>Waiting time explodes → performance down</li></ul>
<p>If very few lines require synchronization, never recommended to declare entire method synchronized. Enclose those few lines in a synchronized block — recommended practice.</p>
<p>Advantage of synchronized block over synchronized method: performance improves (waiting time reduced). Outside the block, multiple threads can run freely; only the small critical section is exclusive.</p>
<p>Rule of thumb:</p>
<ul><li>Few lines need sync → synchronized block (local)</li></ul>
<ul><li>Whole method needs sync → declare method synchronized (global to that method)</li></ul>
<h3>08:22 — Hyderabad → Vijayawada / narrow bridge analogy</h3>
<p>Hyderabad to Vijayawada ~300 km (~6 hours old days / ~4 hours now).</p>
<p>In the middle (Kodad area): a narrow bridge — at a time only one vehicle.</p>
<p>Worst government rule analogy: “because of that bridge, only one vehicle allowed for the entire Hyderabad–Vijayawada journey” → ~4 vehicles/day → lifetime may not reach Vijayawada.</p>
<p>Correct: many vehicles on the highway; only the bridge portion is synchronized. Wait maybe ~1 minute at the bridge, not 6 hours for the whole route.</p>
<p>Same moral: don’t sync 300 km when only the bridge needs mutual exclusion.</p>
<h3>13:26 — Board dictation: advantage</h3>
<pre class='code'>/*
If very few lines of the code required synchronization
then it is not recommended to declare entire method as synchronized
We have to enclose those few lines of the code by using synchronized block

Main advantage of synchronized block over synchronized method:
  it reduces waiting time of threads
  and improves performance of the system / application
*/</pre>
<h3>15:48 — How to declare synchronized block (3 forms)</h3>
<p>1) Lock of current object — `synchronized(this)`</p>
<p>synchronized (this) {
    // if a thread got lock of current object
    // then only it is allowed to execute this area
}</p>
<p>2) Lock of a particular object `b`</p>
<p>synchronized (b) {
    // if a thread got lock of particular object b
    // then only it is allowed to execute this area
}</p>
<p>3) Class-level lock — `synchronized(Display.class)`</p>
<p>synchronized (Display.class) {
    // if a thread got class level lock of Display class
    // then only it is allowed to execute this area
}</p>
<p>Display.class represents the Class object of Display — its lock is the class-level lock.</p>
<h3>25:03 — Same wish demo, but only the for-loop in a block</h3>
<p>Assume wish has one lakh lines before the loop and one lakh lines after — only the for-loop needs sync.</p>
<pre class='code'>class Display {
    public void wish(String name) {
        // ..... 1 lakh lines of code .....
        // (multiple threads OK here)

        synchronized (this) {
            for (int i = 0; i &lt; 10; i++) {
                System.out.print(&quot;Good Morning: &quot;);
                try {
                    Thread.sleep(2000);
                } catch (InterruptedException e) {
                }
                System.out.println(name);
            }
        }

        // ..... another 1 lakh lines of code .....
        // (multiple threads OK here)
    }
}

class MyThread extends Thread {
    Display d;
    String name;

    MyThread(Display d, String name) {
        this.d = d;
        this.name = name;
    }

    public void run() {
        d.wish(name);
    }
}

class SynchronizedDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Display d = new Display();
        MyThread t1 = new MyThread(d, &quot;Dhoni&quot;);
        MyThread t2 = new MyThread(d, &quot;Yuvi&quot;);
        t1.start();
        t2.start();
    }
}</pre>
<p>Without any synchronized keyword → irregular output.</p>
<p>Two ways to fix:</p>
<ol><li>Declare entire wish synchronized → syncs 2 lakh + loop (unnecessary wait on the huge non-critical parts)</li></ol>
<ol><li>`synchronized(this)` around only the for-loop → regular output for the wish cycle, better performance</li></ol>
<p>He runs with block → regular output (Good Morning: Dhoni × 10 then Yuvi, or reverse).</p>
<h3>41:29 — Change: two Display objects + `synchronized(this)`</h3>
<pre class='code'>class SynchronizedDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Display d1 = new Display();
        Display d2 = new Display();
        MyThread t1 = new MyThread(d1, &quot;Dhoni&quot;);
        MyThread t2 = new MyThread(d2, &quot;Yuvi&quot;);
        t1.start();
        t2.start();
    }
}</pre>
<p>With synchronized(this) inside wish:</p>
<ul><li>For T1, current object = d1</li></ul>
<ul><li>For T2, current object = d2</li></ul>
<ul><li>Different locks → both can enter their blocks simultaneously → irregular output</li></ul>
<h3>45:37 — Fix with class-level lock in the block</h3>
<p>Requirement: still regular even with two Display objects → need class-level lock:</p>
<pre class='code'>class Display {
    public void wish(String name) {
        // ..... 1 lakh lines .....

        synchronized (Display.class) {
            for (int i = 0; i &lt; 10; i++) {
                System.out.print(&quot;Good Morning: &quot;);
                try {
                    Thread.sleep(2000);
                } catch (InterruptedException e) {
                }
                System.out.println(name);
            }
        }

        // ..... 1 lakh lines .....
    }
}</pre>
<p>Only one class-level lock for Display. First thread takes it; second waits → regular output even when d1 ≠ d2.</p>
<h3>47:50 — Important conclusion: no lock for primitives</h3>
<p>Java has two kinds of locks: object-level and class-level. Lock applies to objects and classes — not to primitive values.</p>
<pre class='code'>class Test {
    public void m1() {
        int x = 10;
        synchronized (x) {
            // ...
        }
        // CE: unexpected type
        // found   : int
        // required: reference
    }
}
/*
Lock concept applicable for object types and class types
but not for primitives

Hence we can&#x27;t pass primitive type as argument to synchronized block
otherwise we will get compile time error saying
unexpected type found int required reference
*/</pre>
<h3>53:04 — FAQs (interview / SCJP style)</h3>
<p>He lists FAQs and expected answers:</p>
<p>1. What is synchronized keyword? Where can we apply?</p>
<p>Modifier applicable for methods and blocks, not for variables and classes.</p>
<p>2. Advantage of synchronized?</p>
<p>Resolve data inconsistency problems.</p>
<p>3. Disadvantage?</p>
<p>Increases waiting time of threads and creates performance problems.</p>
<p>4. What is race condition?</p>
<p>(He says he covered the concept without always naming the word.)</p>
<p>If multiple threads operate simultaneously on the same Java object and cause data inconsistency → race condition. Overcome with synchronized modifier/keyword.</p>
<pre class='code'>/*
If multiple threads are operating simultaneously on same Java object
then there may be a chance of data inconsistency problem
This is called race condition
We can overcome this problem by using synchronized keyword
*/</pre>
<p>5. What is object lock and when required?</p>
<p>Every object has a unique lock (object lock). Required when a thread wants to execute instance synchronized method (or sync block on that object).</p>
<p>6. What is class-level lock and when required?</p>
<p>Every class has a unique lock. Required when a thread wants to execute static synchronized method (or synchronized(ClassName.class)).</p>
<p>7. Difference class-level vs object-level lock?</p>
<p>Static synchronized → class lock; instance synchronized → object lock.</p>
<p>8. While a thread executes synchronized method on an object, can remaining threads execute any other synchronized method on same object simultaneously?</p>
<p>No.</p>
<p>9. What is synchronized block?</p>
<p>(As taught.)</p>
<p>10. How to declare block for current-object lock?</p>
<p>synchronized(this) { }</p>
<p>11. How to declare block for class-level lock?</p>
<p>synchronized(Display.class) { } (any ClassName.class)</p>
<p>12. Advantage of synchronized block over method?</p>
<p>Performance improved; waiting time reduced.</p>
<h3>1:03:19 — Can a thread acquire multiple locks simultaneously?</h3>
<p>Answer: Yes — from different objects.</p>
<pre class='code'>class X {
    public synchronized void m1() {
        Y y = new Y();
        synchronized (y) {
            Z z = new Z();
            synchronized (z) {
                // ...
            }
        }
    }
}

class Y { }
class Z { }

class Demo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        X x = new X();
        x.m1();
        // thread needs lock of x to enter m1
        // then lock of y to enter synchronized(y)
        // then lock of z to enter synchronized(z)
        // so thread holds locks of x, y, and z (different objects)
    }
}</pre>
<p>Board checkpoints he marks:</p>
<pre class='code'>// thread has lock of X object
// synchronized (y) → thread has locks of X and Y
// synchronized (z) → thread has locks of X, Y, Z
/*
Is a thread can acquire multiple locks simultaneously?
Yes of course from different objects
*/</pre>
<h3>1:09:56 — What is “synchronized statement”?</h3>
<p>As per Java specification, there is no such official terminology. Interview people coined it.</p>
<p>According to them: statements present inside a synchronized method or synchronized block are called synchronized statements — statements that can be executed by only one thread at a time (for that lock).</p>
<pre class='code'>/*
Interview people created terminology:
The statements present in synchronized method
and synchronized block
are called synchronized statements
*/</pre>
<h3>1:12:28 — Close</h3>
<p>Clarity expected: synchronized method vs block; object vs class lock; when block is preferred; FAQ set including race condition and multiple locks.</p>
